PURPOSE
Evaluate trajectories of a device moving in a flow field, including where it
starts, whether and when it reaches a destination, and the conditions or
consumption associated with the journey.

IDENTIFIER CONVENTION
Requirement identifiers use REQ-<SCOPE>-<TOPIC>. SCOPE is GEN for general
requirements, DRONE for the city-drone scenario, PILL for the pill-in-pipe
scenario, and NOFLOW for flow-independent route generation. TOPIC describes
the requirement subject. Identifiers are independent of requirement ordering
and should remain unchanged when requirements are inserted or reordered.

GENERAL REQUIREMENTS

REQ-GEN-FLOW
The program shall evaluate device trajectories in a supplied or calculated
flow field.
Implementation: Provide an OpenFOAM application in src/fslFoam. The application
is run with an analysis case, reads its input dictionary from
analysis_case_folder/system/fsStreamLineDict, and reads the OpenFOAM case to
analyze from the dictionary's "case" entry. Resolve a relative "case" path
relative to the analysis case; accept an absolute path as-is. Read the mesh and
velocity field U from the latest numeric time directory in the referenced case.
Classes involved: main() in fslFoam.C coordinates case setup and
functionObjects::streamlines generates trajectories. StreamlineVtkReader reads
the generated VTK into TrackData.

REQ-GEN-REGIONS
The program shall accept one or more start patches and one or more destination
patches. Domain regions are not supported.
Implementation: Read the patch names from "startPatches" and
"destinationPatches" in fsStreamLineDict. Each entry is a list of patch names;
default to "(inlet)" for startPatches and "(outlet)" for destinationPatches.
Reject any configured patch name that does not exist in the analyzed mesh.
Classes involved: No dedicated class yet; main() reads the patch lists and
validates them against the mesh boundary.

REQ-GEN-START-LOCATIONS
The program shall evaluate trajectories from multiple candidate starting
locations within the specified start region and identify the starting
locations that meet the scenario's destination and performance criteria.
Implementation: Randomly sample points over the configured start patches,
with sampling probability proportional to patch-face area. "nPoints" specifies
the total number of seeds across all configured start patches.
Classes involved: No dedicated class yet; main() configures
functionObjects::streamlines with the boundaryRandom seed sample set.

REQ-MOTION-SPACE
The program shall constrain each evaluated device trajectory to a configured
height range and enforce a configured minimum clearance from buildings, trees,
and other obstacle geometry. A trajectory that leaves the height range or
violates the clearance requirement shall not be considered feasible.
Implementation: Configure the minimum and maximum heights and obstacle
clearance for the scenario using "minHeight", "maxHeight",
"minObstacleClearance", and "obstaclePatches" in fsStreamLineDict. Height is
the z coordinate. The height limits default to unbounded and obstacle
clearance defaults to zero, imposing no restriction. Check each point in the
generated streamline against the configured limits; a positive clearance
requires one or more named obstacle boundary patches. Report paths that
violate a constraint as out_of_corridor. This flow-streamline application
does not currently model stuck device motion.
Classes involved: MotionCorridorConstraint checks height and obstacle
clearance. StreamlineSummaryWriter applies it to each parsed track and reports
the resulting outcome; scenario-specific trajectory evaluators remain future
work.

REQ-GEN-OUTCOMES
The program shall report trajectory outcomes, including destination
attainment and travel time, for the evaluated starting locations.
Implementation: Track each streamline until it first intersects a boundary.
It reaches its destination only if that first boundary is one of the configured
destination patches; stop tracking at that intersection. Always write a
per-streamline summary CSV for all evaluated paths under the analysis case's
postProcessing/ directory sorted ascending by the TTF (total time of flight).
For each path, record a stable streamline/seed ID,
start and end coordinates, TTF, whether a configured destination was reached,
the outcome (success, out_of_corridor, other_boundary, or tracking_limit),
and the destination patch name if reached. A path is successful only if it
reaches a configured destination and remains within the motion corridor.
Write
streamline geometry in VTK format under the same analysis postProcessing/
directory only when "outputStreamLines on" is set in fsStreamLineDict; VTK
output is off by default.
Classes involved: Track and TrackData hold the parsed data;
StreamlineVtkReader reads VTK; StreamlineSummaryWriter writes the CSV,
classifies the ending boundary, and applies MotionCorridorConstraint.
main() controls optional VTK file copying.

REQ-GEN-DEVICE-MODELS
The program shall support scenario-specific device models: a controlled
vehicle model for the drone scenario and a physical-motion model for the pill
scenario.
Classes involved: Not implemented. Future DroneControlModel and
PillMotionModel classes will provide scenario-specific motion.

USER STORY 1: DRONE IN THE CITY

REQ-DRONE-OPENFOAM-FLOW
The program shall support a city flow field calculated using OpenFOAM.
Classes involved: main() loads the OpenFOAM case and invokes
functionObjects::streamlines; there is no scenario-specific class.

REQ-DRONE-CITY-GEOMETRY
The program shall support city-building geometry harvested from open sources.
Classes involved: Not implemented; no geometry-import class exists yet.

REQ-DRONE-CONTROL-MODEL
The program shall model the drone using a control model.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned DroneControlModel.

REQ-DRONE-FASTEST-ARRIVAL
Given a specified start patch and destination domain or patch, the program
shall evaluate drone trajectories from candidate locations on the start patch
and identify the location or locations with the fastest arrival at the
destination.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned DroneTrajectoryEvaluator will
compare candidate outcomes. StreamlineSummaryWriter currently writes generic
trajectory outcomes only.

REQ-DRONE-BATTERY-CONSUMPTION
The program shall report battery consumption for each evaluated drone
trajectory and for the fastest-arrival result.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned DroneBatteryModel.

USER STORY 2: PILL IN THE CHAMBER

REQ-PILL-STL-GEOMETRY
The program shall accept geometry supplied as an STL file.
Classes involved: Not implemented; a future StlGeometryReader would own STL
input if this requirement is brought into scope.

REQ-PILL-START-LOCATIONS
Given a specified start domain or patch and destination domain or patch, the
program shall evaluate pill trajectories from candidate starting locations.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned PillTrajectoryEvaluator. The
current main() seed setup handles generic flow streamlines on patches only.

REQ-PILL-PHYSICAL-MODEL
The program shall model pill motion according to the applicable physical
laws.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned PillMotionModel.

REQ-PILL-TIME-LIMIT
The program shall use 100 times the fluid residence time in the domain as the
maximum evaluation time for deciding whether the pill reaches its destination
in a reasonable time.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned PillTrajectoryEvaluator will apply
the scenario time limit.

REQ-PILL-REACHABLE-START-LOCATIONS
The program shall identify starting locations from which the pill reaches
the destination within the maximum evaluation time.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned PillTrajectoryEvaluator.

REQ-PILL-STUCK-LOCATIONS
The program shall identify starting locations where the pill becomes stuck,
defined as its trajectory velocity falling below a specified low-velocity
criterion. The criterion shall be an input to the evaluation.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned PillMotionModel will expose the
velocity state and PillTrajectoryEvaluator will apply the configured
criterion.

REQ-PILL-TRAJECTORY-OUTCOMES
The program shall report the travel time or stuck outcome for each evaluated
pill starting location.
Classes involved: Not implemented; planned PillTrajectoryReporter. The
current StreamlineSummaryWriter reports generic flow-streamline outcomes,
not pill-specific stuck states.

REQ-NOFLOW-ZRM
When the analysis dictionary contains a "ZermelloTrjNoFlow" block, the
program shall generate no-flow device routes independently of the
flow-following streamlines. In the first implementation, each route shall lie
in a horizontal plane at the midpoint of finite, configured minHeight and
maxHeight values. If variableHeight is enabled, the program shall use a 3D
route constrained to that height range; until 3D routing is implemented, it
shall reject this setting rather than silently use a 2D route.
Implementation: Use the existing startPatches and destinationPatches settings.
Intersect these boundary patches with the route plane. Sample start points
along the start-patch intersections, weighted by segment length, using
nPoints. Require an obstaclePatches list naming boundary patches whose
intersection with the route plane forms closed obstacle footprints. Require a
positive deviceRadius; allow an optional, non-negative obstacleMargin,
defaulting to zero. Expand obstacle footprints by deviceRadius plus
obstacleMargin. For each start point, find the nearest point on any reachable
destination-patch intersection and route to it along the shortest
collision-free piecewise-linear path around the expanded footprints, using a
visibility graph. The route shall not depend on the flow velocity field.
Compute route length from its waypoints and travel time using deviceSpeed,
defaulting to 0.1 m/s; configured speed shall be positive.
Write successful routes to output separate from streamline results, recording
a stable route ID, start and end coordinates, waypoints, route length, and
travel time. If no feasible route exists for a start, omit its route geometry
and log the unreachable start. The 3D variable-height route algorithm is
future work; enabling it before it is implemented shall produce a clear
configuration error.
Classes involved: ZermelloTrajectoryGenerator is the abstract base class and
owns common configuration and validation, route-space and obstacle
preparation, patch intersection and start sampling, destination selection,
shared result collection, travel-time calculation, and unreachable-start
logging. It defines the generation workflow and an overridable route-building
strategy. ZermelloTrjNoFlow derives from it and implements the 2D
visibility-graph strategy for shortest collision-free routes in the
fixed-height plane. ZermelloConstantFlow is a planned derived generator for a
constant-flow route strategy; its flow-dependent route behavior is not
specified here. ZermelloRouteWriter writes Zermello results separately from
streamline output.